1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Welcome back to the channel, this is the Q1 energy and infrastructure outlook.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Before we get into it, a quick reminder that none of this is financial advice.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Last quarter we talked about interconnect queues and I got a lot of questions about that.

4
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:48,000
So let's start with what actually happened, then I'll get into where I think it goes.

5
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The utility published its integrated resource plan in February and it runs to four hundred pages.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,000
I read the whole thing so you don't have to, and the short version is: more gas, later coal retirements.

7
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The interesting part is the load forecast, which they revised up for the third year running.

8
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Most of that revision is attributed to large loads, which is their polite term for data centers.

9
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Here's my first call for the year: by the end of next year, Illinois regulators will approve at least two new gigawatt-scale data center campuses.

10
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:59,000
Now, the county side. Three rezoning cases came up in the winter and two of them were tabled.

11
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:11,000
One of them was in the same township we covered in the November episode.

12
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Neighbors showed up with the noise study from the site two counties over, and that carried the room.

13
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:35,000
I'm not going to relitigate that here; you can watch the November episode if you want the detail.

14
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Let's look at the mining side, because hashprice has been ugly for six months.

15
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Difficulty adjusted up again, and the miners with the oldest fleets are the ones feeling it.

16
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:11,000
I talked to two operators last week and both are running curtailment programs they didn't have last year.

17
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Curtailment revenue is real money now, and that changes the math on where you site a facility.

18
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Okay, the transmission piece. The regional operator approved the capacity auction results in December.

19
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Prices cleared much higher than the previous auction, which everyone expected after last year's numbers.

20
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:59,000
The part that surprised me was how much of that is being passed straight through to industrial customers.

21
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:11,000
If you're an industrial customer on a demand charge, your bill looks very different than it did two years ago.

22
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Let me pull up the chart. This is the capacity price series going back a decade.

23
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:35,000
You can see the step change; that's not a trend line, that's a regime change.

24
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:47,000
A viewer asked whether I think the auction rules get reformed. I don't know, and I'm not going to pretend I do.

25
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:59,000
What I can tell you is that the stakeholder process is scheduled to run through the summer.

26
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Now, water. This came up in three of the county hearings and it did not come up at all two years ago.

27
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:23,000
The proposals I've seen are mostly about disclosure, not limits, at least so far.

28
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Moving to the state house. There were four bills filed this session that touch large loads.

29
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Two of them are essentially the same bill filed in both chambers, so call it three real proposals.

30
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:59,000
One of them would create a separate rate class for facilities above a certain size.

31
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Another would require a study before any new interconnect above a size threshold is approved.

32
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:23,000
None of them have had a committee hearing yet, and the session calendar is short this year.

33
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:35,000
For background, this is the same committee that killed a similar bill in 2024 without a vote.

34
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Alright, the federal piece is short because not much moved this quarter.

35
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:52,000
I'll be careful with this one. Hashprice might recover to eighty dollars a petahash within six months, if difficulty stalls.

36
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:04,000
There was an executive order in January about siting on federal land, and agencies are still writing guidance.

37
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:16,000
I'll cover that in detail when the guidance actually drops; speculating now isn't useful.

38
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Back to the mining economics for a minute, because this is where most of you live.

39
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Fleet efficiency is the whole game right now; if you're above thirty joules per terahash you're in trouble.

40
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:52,000
The secondary market for last-generation machines is flooded, and prices reflect that.

41
00:07:52,000 --> 00:08:04,000
I've seen units go for less than the shipping cost, which tells you where the market is.

42
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Somebody in the comments asked about immersion. Yes, it helps, but it doesn't fix an old fleet.

43
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Let's talk about the campus we toured in the fall, because there's an update.

44
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:40,000
They finished the substation work and energized the first forty megawatts in January.

45
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:52,000
The second phase is waiting on transformers, which is the same story everywhere.

46
00:08:52,000 --> 00:09:04,000
Transformer lead times are still measured in years, and that's the real bottleneck, not permits.

47
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Question from the live chat: do I think the tariff changes affect transformer pricing? Probably, but I haven't seen numbers.

48
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Okay, let me wrap the quarter with what I'm watching.

49
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:40,000
First, the committee calendar; if any of the large-load bills get a hearing, that's a signal.

50
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Second, the guidance on federal siting, which I expect to be thin on specifics.

51
00:09:52,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Third, whether the operators I talked to actually expand or just keep curtailing.

52
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:16,000
That's the outlook. Links to the resource plan and the auction results are in the description.

53
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:28,000
If you made it this far, thanks; the long episodes are the ones that get the least views and the most email.

54
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:40,000
I'll be back next month with the county hearing recap and, hopefully, some transformer news.

